UtilsError  --> TransportError : From, exhaustive
CryptoError -[#B91C1C]-> TransportError : From, exhaustive\n**logs**
DmCoreError --> NodeCoreError : From, exhaustive

' ======================================================================
' L4 — the data model layer and its local trait errors
' ======================================================================

package "L4  rs-matter-dm" #F8FAFF {

  enum DmError {
    ClusterUnsupported
    CommandUnsupported
    ...
    == the ONLY payload variant ==
    Status(IMStatusCode)
    ..
    **1 byte**
  }

  enum NetCtlError <<trait error — LOCAL>> #FEF9C3 {
    NetworkNotFound
    UnsupportedSecurity
    AuthFailure
    OtherConnectionFailure
    IpBindFailed
    IpV6Failed
    ..
    fieldless -> **1 byte**
    ..
    **Consumed by its own handler.**
    Never collated upward.
  }

  enum PushAvError <<hook error — LOCAL>> #FEF9C3 {
    == IM status subset ==
    Status(PushAvStatus)
    == cluster-specific codes ==
    Cluster(StatusCodeEnum)
    ..
    2 payload variants -> 2 bytes,
    confined to one hook API
  }

  interface ClusterStatus {
    + to_status() : (IMStatusCode, Option<NonZeroU8>)
  }

  class HookResultExt {
    + stage(sink) : Result<T, DmError>
    + stage_into(sink) : DmError
    ..
    blanket impl for
    Result<T, E: ClusterStatus>
  }
}

DmCoreError    --> DmError : From, exhaustive
TransportError -[#B91C1C]-> DmError : From, exhaustive\n**logs**
NodeCoreError  --> DmError : From, exhaustive
PushAvError ..|> ClusterStatus
NetCtlError ..|> ClusterStatus
HookResultExt ..> ClusterStatus : reads via
HookResultExt --> DmError : returns Status(Failure)\n+ stages the cluster byte

note bottom of NetCtlError
  **Trait and hook errors do not propagate.**
  Each is consumed by the handler that called
  it, mapped to a cluster response field or
  staged as a cluster status. They never reach
  DmError or the root -- which is what lets
  DmError keep exactly one payload variant.
end note

' ======================================================================
' The collator — StackError. There is NO extra root error.
' ======================================================================

package "L5  rs-matter-stack  (owns the top-level run loop)" #FFF7ED {

  enum StackError {
    == its own failures: only ONE today ==
    ...
    == almost entirely From targets ==
    Utils(UtilsError)
    Tlv(TlvError)
    Crypto(CryptoError)
    DmCore(DmCoreError)
    Transport(TransportError)
    NodeCore(NodeCoreError)
    Dm(DmError)
    ... one per crate it collates
    -- **set TBD**
    --
    + status() : Option<IMStatusCode>
    ..
    ~12 payload variants -> **2 bytes**
  }
}

UtilsError     --> StackError : From
TlvError       --> StackError : From
CryptoError    --> StackError : From
DmCoreError    --> StackError : From
TransportError --> StackError : From
NodeCoreError  --> StackError : From
DmError        --> StackError : From

note left of StackError
  **No separate root error in rs-matter.**
  The facade has zero Result returns, so a
  collator there would have no users. The
  collating need is real but it lives HERE:
  `Stack::run()` / `startup()` can fail with
  anything from transport, sc, dm, node or
  crypto, so whatever they return IS the
  collator. rs-matter just re-exports it.

  Examples already show the shape:
  `fn main() -> Result<(), Error>` with `?`
  on `stack.startup(..)`.

  **This crate is the INVERSE of every other
  one.** It has 1 distinct ErrorCode variant
  and 2 mentions of its own, so StackError is
  almost all From targets. Its enumeration
  question is not "what can this layer fail
  with" but "what can reach this layer".

  2 bytes is fine: one type, at the outermost
  boundary, never on a hot path.
  ----
  **thiserror no longer has a home here.**
  It looked right when the collator sat in a
  std-side facade. rs-matter-stack is
  compiled FOR THE DEVICE, so ~12 Display
  strings land in flash duplicating what
  defmt already interns. Keep thiserror to
  rs-matter-codegen (a host-side tool) and
  nowhere else.
  ----
  **Why status() and not a Status variant.**
  A status arrives inside whichever crate
  error carried it. A top-level
  StackError::Status(..) would make it
  reachable two ways, and a `match` handling
  one would silently miss the other. The
  accessor has no invariant to break.
end note

' ======================================================================
' Rules and measurements
' ======================================================================

note as N_SIZE
  **MEASURED (rustc -O, size_of)**

  fieldless enum, any variant count <= 256   1
  one payload variant (Status(IMStatusCode)) 1
  TWO payload variants                       2
  root with ~10 payload variants             2
  nesting depth 3 / depth 4                  1
  flattened equivalent                       1

  **Depth is free. Breadth costs.**
  Flattening a nested error buys ZERO bytes.

  **Data size is unchanged by this design;
  code size rises slightly** -- each boundary
  is an N-arm match rather than a move, and
  total variant count across all layers will
  be ~2-3x today's 79. Accepted.
end note

note as N_RULES
  **Rules**

  1. **No escape hatch.** No Other(Error),
     no Internal(..). A layer's enum lists
     only what that layer can fail with.

  2. **At most ONE payload-carrying variant**
     per enum keeps it at 1 byte. For layers
     that report status, that variant is
     Status(IMStatusCode).

  3. **Boundary conversions are From impls
     with an exhaustive match and NO `_`
     arm.** `?` is then fine. A conversion
     that needs a catch-all is not yet
     designed -- give it a name instead.

  4. **Log where context is lost.** If a
     conversion collapses several inputs onto
     one output, it logs at the boundary. The
     detail belongs in the log, not carried
     up the stack.

  5. **Adding a variant low down ripples.**
     Every consumer's exhaustive match
     breaks. That is the compiler working --
     resist the temptation to add `_`.

  6. **Trait/hook errors stay local.** They
     are consumed by their handler and never
     collated.
end note

note as N_NEXT
  **NEXT: specify the variant lists.**
  Every variant list in this diagram is
  illustrative. Enumerating them IS the work,
  and the only place this design can fail: if
  one layer is under-specified, its
  conversions start collapsing and the escape
  hatch returns by a longer road, with the
  loss invisible instead of named.

  Suggested order -- hardest first, so a
  failure is cheap:
    rs-matter-dm         1681 Results
    rs-matter-transport   412 Results
  then tlv (275), im-core (197),
  dm-core (162), node-core (155),
  stack (152), im (147), sc (95), bdx (47).
end note

